## Contents
## 1. Introduction
Intrinsic high stereoselectivity, catalytic performance, safe nature, and mild operating conditions constitute biocatalysis a prevalent choice over the use of chemical catalysts [1–3]. Lipases (EC 3.1.1.3) are omnipresent biocatalysts that hydrolyze triacylglycerols to glycerols and free fatty acids. As emerging key enzymes, they are extensively utilized in a wide array of biotechnological applications such as dairy, food technology, detergent, fine chemical, and pharmaceutical industries due to their huge potential and multifaceted properties [4–6]. It is also well recognized that lipases are among the most frequently used enzymes in organic synthesis and are typically involved in over $20\%$ of biotransformation reactions [7]. However, like other enzymes, native lipases also suffer from certain limitations such as low stability under operative conditions and the impossibility of reuse. Moreover, they also tend to form aggregates that diminish their catalytic activity. Comparative to free counterparts, insolubilized enzymes partake improved operational characteristics under noteworthy conditions such as highly acidic/ basic pH environment, weak interaction, extreme temperature (thermal environment), etc. [8,9]. The factors mentioned above considerably affect the catalytic cycle. A generalized scheme of the lipase catalytic cycle is shown in Fig. 1.
Immobilization of lipases onto appropriate support materials might enhance the industrial performance and economy by surpassing the inadequacies as mentioned above [10]. Support matrices can meritoriously distribute enzyme molecules, circumvents aggregation, and retain their active forms. Immobilized counterparts can also be retrieved from the reaction system and reprocessed in multiple cycles. After attachment to a solid support, the pH, thermal and operational stability of the enzyme is usually increased compared to the free form of enzymes [8,11,12].
Recent advances in nanotechnology have provided a plethora of diverse nanoarchitectures and nanocaffolds that have gained growing interest to substantiate enzyme immobilization. Nanostructured materials as a support matrix offer potential advantages than conventional immobilization counterparts by offering various advantages such as high surface area, high mass transfer rate, better stability, and low diffusivity [13–15]. Immobilization of biocatalysts on nanostructured materials could considerably enhance their durability, functional stability, and recycling ability, as well as the easy recovery and separation [16,17]. This article presents a state-of-art and prospects of nanostructured materials as novel paradigms for the development of immobilized lipases-based biocatalytic systems to improve their properties for biotechnology applications. The information covered here would be beneficial to expand the application prospects of immobilized lipase onto nanostructures and appealing to a wide range of readers with biotechnological backgrounds.
## 2. Graphene oxide for lipase immobilization
Graphene oxide (GO) has recently fascinated increasing attention for lipases immobilization. Indeed, GO is a derivative of graphene that is obtained readily from natural graphite by strong oxidation and exfoliation steps [18]. A set of interesting attributes that include a considerable surface area for enzyme binding, abundant surface functionalities (i.e., epoxy, hydroxyl or carboxyl groups) for easy modification, great mechanical and chemical strength, good biocompatibility, and stabilized dispersion in the aqueous phase for immobilizing hydrophilic enzymes makes GO as a prodigious support carrier for the immobilization of lipases (Table 1) [19,20]. The presence of numerous surface groups is the key chemical skeletons that create immobilization regions for lipases, and thus enable robust lipase coupling via electrostatic interactions or chemical linkages. Importantly, primary amino groups of lipases react with carboxyl groups on the surface of GO by amidation chemistry [19–21]. Therefore, the functionalization of the GO surface is meaningful for the efficient immobilization of lipase. Zhang et al. [22,23] used (3-mercaptopropyl) trimethoxysilane-functionalized GO for the immobilization of lipase to hydrolyze $p$ -nitrophenyl palmitate. The modified GO-bound lipase expressed high catalytic efficiency $(93.4\%)$ compared to the soluble counterpart. The reuse capability was $69.9\%$ after 10 consecutive times. Intending to circumvent the use of hazardous agents for chemical reduction of GO, Zhuang et al. [24] applied disodium guanosine $5^{\prime}$ -monophosphate (GMP-2Na) as a suitable reductant to fabricate functionalized GO for efficient lipase attachment (Fig. 2). After the immobilization process, the immobilized lipase achieved a 2-fold greater activity than that of the free enzyme. It also demonstrated improved durability because the activity was not reduced after incubating for $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $\mathsf{p H}6.0$ and 7.0. After preservation for more than one month, the residual activity of GO-immobilized lipase was $54.47\%$ greater than the native enzyme. Immobilization of lipase from Brevibacillus borstelensis onto glutaraldehyde-functionalized GO exerted a constructive and significant impact on the thermal stability of lipase at an elevated temperature of $95^{\circ}C$ over a wide range of alkaline pH ( $\mathrm{\dot{\pH}}7$ to 12 36) [25]. Exfoliated GO by the use of 3-aminopropyl triethoxysilane appeared as elegant support for CRL immobilization to synthesize ester ethyl caprylate. Experimental results revealed a substantial enhancement in the thermal steadiness, and the enzyme activity increased to $80\%$ at $40^{\circ}C$ after immobilization, while it was $58\%$ in the case of free lipase [21].
Xie and Huang [26] prepared GO nanosheets by following the strong oxidation, sonication, and centrifugation steps. Though the surface of the GO can be enriched with plenty of active groups (such as hydroxyl, carboxyl, and epoxy groups) by the oxidation process. Nevertheless, the GO sheets are agglomerated because of the strong π-π stacking between the sheets, leading to a reduction in surface area and covering of functional moieties. In an attempt to prevent the GO aggregation, MNPs can be introduced into GO by a chemical co-precipitation method to integrate the advantages of substantial surface area and high magnetization. In this context, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ MNPs were first encapsulated within GO skeleton, and the resultant magnetic GO preparation was applied as easily separable magnetic support for lipase immobilization to develop an environmentally sustainable and efficient biocatalytic system. The enzyme was attached via amide connections in the presence of EDC as a suitable activating candidate (Fig. 3) [26]. The exploitation of covalent binding for lipase immobilization not only evades the enzyme desorption but also magnifies the enzymatic durability. The GO-immobilized enzyme was easily separated using a magnetic field and allowed the biocatalyst to be recycled five consecutive times without losing its significant activity. In fact, typically hydrophilic edges of GO bear carboxylic acid groups, which are involved in the lipase immobilization [20]. The centers of GO are more hydrophobic that might interfacially activate the lipase, and the hydrophobic character also prevents glycerin adsorption on the support imparting augmented operational stability to biocatalyst [27,28]. In another study, MGO was modified with 3- aminopropyl triethoxysilane, as well as a combination of glutaraldehyde and 3-aminopropyl triethoxysilane to immobilize Rhizopus oryzae lipase using both the electrostatic interactions and covalent linkages (Fig. 4) [29]. ROL/MGO-AP-GA biocatalytic system attained the maximum biodiesel conversion of $71.19\%$ and retained
$58.77\%$ of its original catalytic activity after five continuous transesterification reaction cycles. The kinetic parameters, loading capacity, storage, and thermal stability were improved after the modification of magnetic GO [29]. Jafarian et al. [30] synthesized GO nanosheets (GON) and functionalized with 2, 4,6-trichloro-1,3,5-triazine (TCT) via two methods. In the first route, GON was directly reacted with TCT (designated as GON-1), while GON was allowed to react with 3- aminopropyltriethoxysilane (APTS)-functionalized TCT in the second route (GON-2). Afterward, bare GO and functionalized GO (GON-1 and GON-2) were applied as support matrices for CRL immobilization (Fig. 5) [30]. Immobilized CRL represented the marked shelf life and equitable recyclability; however, the thermal and pH stability of CRL $@$ GON-2 were greater than others. It was observed that the long linker of GON-2 prevented the negative interaction between enzyme/enzyme and enzyme/product, leading to enhanced pH and thermal stability as well as catalytic performance [30].
## 3. Carbon nanotubes for lipase immobilization
Carbon nanotubes (CNTs) have gained more and more consideration as an incredibly alluring nanosupport for lipase immobilization due to large surface area, unique morphologies, profound chemical stability, and biocompatible and mechanical properties [31]. Until now, a plethora of reports have documented the immobilization of lipase from various microbial sources onto single (SWCNTs) as well as multi-walled carbon nanotubes (MWCNTs) (Table 2). In earlier reports, enzymes, e.g., lipase were immobilized by simple adsorption (Figure 6) [7]. However, the plausibility of surface functionalization of CNTs unveiled new opportunities for covalent attachment of enzymes. Results demonstrate that covalent attachment showed 2-times enzyme loading than simple adsorption under the optimal conditions. Likewise, covalently immobilized preparations also had three times greater enzyme activity. Lipase from Pseudomonas fluorescens was covalently attached to functionalized MWCNTs to develop a nanobiocatalyst that promotes kinetic resolution of (RS)-1-phenyl ethanol into (S)-1-phenyl ethanol. Immobilized nanopreparation exhibited 10-fold improvement in catalytic activity, thermostability (up to a high temperature of $80~^{\circ}C)$ , and recycling ability (up to eight continuous cycles). PFL was covalently conjugated onto various carboxylic functional groups bearing MWCNTs in two sequential steps via diimide-activated amidation chemistry since it circumvents the intermolecular aggregation of the enzyme [1]. Nurullina et al. [32] immobilized a recombinant lipase by adsorption on unmodified, CNTs, aggregated CNTs, or nitrogen-doped CNTs, and applied to the production of esters in organic solvents at low temperature. By increasing the level of nitrogen introduction into the CNTs, the activity of heterogeneous biocatalysts and the adsorbed enzyme was improved by 1.5–1.6 times. The developed nanobiocatalysts showed high stability in the batch reaction for the synthesis of esters, maintaining about $80\%$ of their original activities after 36 continuous reaction cycles for $720\mathrm{h}$ .
Despite fascinating applications, all of the lipases-immobilized preparations display the drawbacks of tiresome separation due to the nanometer character of CNTs. Imparting magnetic properties to CNTs before immobilization is a useful strategy to solve this issue. This property facilitates the convenient isolation of immobilized lipases from complex reaction media by applying a magnetic field without the requirement of cumbersome centrifugation or filtration steps. In recent years, magnetic CNTs have been fabricated for the attachment of lipase enzymes with manipulative characteristics. For example, Zheng et al. [33] reported the facile development of magnetic MWCNTs for lipase immobilization and applied to synthesize 1,3-dioleoyl-2-palmitoylglycerol-enriched human milk fat alternatives. The magnetic MWCNTs were fabricated by simple mixing of MNPs and MWCNTs, which integrates the properties of both magnetic material and MWCNTs. Immobilization of C. lipolytica lipase on mMWCNTs via cation exchange and hydrophobic interactions led to constitute nanobiocatalyst with much-improved activity, thermal stability, and easier reprocessing than the native counterpart does. Even, it had manipulative stability and superior activity over the use of commercial lipase preparations. Notably, the practicality of CLL@mMWCNTs was also corroborated for the synthesis of OPO-rich TAGs using palm oil as the feedstock. Porcine pancreas lipase immobilized on mMWNTs demonstrated a much higher catalytic activity compared to the free form of the enzyme. Under the optimal reaction environment, an immobilized biocatalytic system presented an outstanding biocatalytic performance for the production of substituted 2H-chromenes. In addition, it also possessed sufficient thermal and operational stability and convenient recyclability in catalyzing the reaction (Fig. 7) [34].
## 4. Silica nanoparticles for lipase immobilization
Mesoporous silicates are prodigious materials for immobilizing enzymes in terms of desired features for enzyme carriers such as larger surface area, well-defined pore structure and geometry, controllable pore size, mechanical and thermal strength, toxicological safe, abundant functional moieties for enzyme coupling, hydrophilicity, and potential regenerability [35,36]. To date, different mesoporous silicas, including SBA-15, MCM-41, and mesocellular foam (MCF) have been fabricated for immobilization purposes [37]. Table 3 portrays a recent list of silica nanoparticles as immobilization supports for lipases, improved biocatalytic features, and potential biotechnological applications. It is demonstrated that mesoporous materials accommodate biomolecules, proteins, and enzymes in their outer surface and inner pores by covalent bonding and entrapment, respectively [38]. Based on the preparation conditions, highly ordered mesoporous silica SBA-15 with a pore size of 5 to $30\mathrm{nm}$ can be fabricated in various forms such as fibers, rods, spheres, and platelets [36]. As compared to earlier prepared mesoporous counterparts, SBA-15 has shown superior hydrothermal and thermal tolerance. Owing to the inimitable textural arrangement, ordered mesoporous silicas constitute a useful model system for monitoring characteristics of the bio-immobilization processes. The pore diameters of mesoporous carriers identical to or larger than lipase are ideal for efficient physical adsorption of lipase. Larger pore size may result in a reduction in enzyme loading and instability by leakage of enzymes from the networks [39]. Babaki et al. [40] synthesized mesoporous SBA-15 nanoparticles and modified with 3-glycidyloxypropyl trimethoxysilane for covalent attachment of lipases from three different microbial sources i.e., Rhizomucor miehei (RML), Thermomyces lanuginosus (TLL), and Candida antarctica (CALB). Characterization revealed an elevated thermal steadiness and high methanol resistance for all the immobilized preparations than that to the free form of the enzyme. The immobilized lipase from T. lanuginosus was highly durable and can be recycled in 20 consecutive catalytic runs with the loss of merely $6\%$ activity. Lipases from C. antarctica and R. miehei also demonstrated noteworthy repeated usability, retaining over $90\%$ of their original activities after 15 and 7 continuous reaction cycles. The octadecylalkyl-functionalized mesoporous-silica nanoparticles with adjustable pore sizes ( $1.6\mathrm{-}13~\mathrm{nm}\rangle$ and high C18 content $({\sim}19\mathrm{wt\%})$ were synthesized. Notably, tailored pore size and increased hydrophobic content were found to be involved in the superior biocatalytic performance of immobilized lipase. Optimal C18-MSNs demonstrates a high loading of $711\ \mathrm{mg/g}$ and 5.23 times increased specific activity compared to the soluble counterpart. After five reuse times, the nanobiocatalyst retained over $90\%$ of its original activity [41].
Zwitterionic polymers are macromolecule that contains an equal amount of cationic and anionic and groups in the polymeric chains. These macromolecules have gathered intense interest in biomedical science and materials engineering due to their marked biocompatibility and anti-biofouling features [16,17,42]. They are also of interest for the modification and immobilization of enzymes to provide stability against environmental stresses. For instance, poly(carboxy betaine methacrylate) (pCBMA)-coated $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ nanoparticles were prepared for the immobilization of porcine pancreatic lipase. The resultant engineered enzyme presented wider temperature and pH tolerance and exhibited improved thermal steadiness [43]. Likewise, poly(glycidyl methacrylate) (pGMA)-modified silica nanoparticle immobilized C. rugosa lipase (CRL) revealed 3-times greater catalytic performance, much higher pH, storage, and thermal stabilities [44]. A zwitterionic copolymer-coated carrier matrix is regarded as a promising candidate for the simultaneous improvement of both the lipase activities and stabilities. To prove this thought, Chen et al. [45] fabricated a series of epoxylated zwitterionic copolymer-embedded novel materials by grafting of zwitterionic sulfobetaine methacrylate (SBMA) and poly (glycidyl methacrylate) (GMA) at various molar ratios onto grafted silica nanoparticle (G50/S50, G100/S0, and G10/S90) for the immobilization of lipase (Fig. 8) [45]. In the zwitterionic copolymers (p(GMA-coSBMA)), the presence of zwitterionic moieties i.e., quaternary amino and sulfonic groups, are involved in protein stabilization, where epoxy groups facilitate the covalent attachment of lipase [44,46]. The loading of the enzyme was augmented with an increase in the content of GMA, whereas the enzyme activity varied with the composition of grafted copolymer. In contrast to p(G100-S0)-CRL, p (G10-S90)-CRL and p(G50-S50)-CRL showed a drastic enhancement in pH stability and thermal tolerance. Among these developed preparations, p(G10- S90)-CRL presented the maximum stability.
## 5. Metal nanoparticles for lipase immobilization
Nanoparticles emerged as an interesting choice of support matrices for immobilizing an array of enzymes owing to their many positive attributes, including substantial enzyme loading capacity, huge surface area, biocompatibility, availability of tools for surface manipulation, and mass transfer resistance [39,47]. In particular, nanoscale particles are considered suitable candidates to overcome diffusion problems, which are often prevalent while dealing with macromolecular substrates [38].
Metallic nanomaterials are a group of nano-entities that contain metal elements such as iron, gold, silver, titanium, copper, manganese, and others. As presented in Table 4, these metal nanoparticles can be used for lipases immobilization to improve their biocatalytic features and biotechnological applications. Among the various kinds of metal nanostructures, gold and silver nanoparticles are of particular relevance and commercialized candidates attributed to their inimitable electrical, photothermal, and optical properties. Gold nanoparticles are considered non-toxic and present biocompatibility and good oxidation tolerance because of optical and chemical properties [48,49]. Their surface can be readily functionalized for task-specific purposes, and thiol ligands could be chemically modified to bind target molecules [22,23,50].
AuNPs have been widely investigated to fabricate enzyme-coated NPs networks. For illustration, 5-mercaptopentyl-hexa-ethylene glycol Nidrossisuccinimmide functionalized AuNPs were found suitable for lipase attachment [51]. Many researchers have utilized click chemistry approaches for the effective conjugation of biomolecules on their surface [52]. Recently, lipases have been covalently immobilized on thiolcoated Ag/Au nanoshells [53]. Venditti et al. [49] adopted a versatile and simple method for immobilizing CRL on hydrophilic AuNPs, which were pre-functionalized with sodium 3-mercapto-1-propane sulfonate (3MPS) and 2-diethylaminoethanethiol hydrochloride (DEA) (Fig. 9). The resultant bio-composites explicitly Au-3MPS@CRL and Au-DEA $@$ CRL, were characterized and compared with the free form of the enzyme. The enzyme stability and activity were substantially ameliorated in the case of the Au-DEA@CRL biocomposite. Au-DEA@CRL manifested a pronounced biocatalytic activity retaining up to $95\%$ of remaining lipolytic activity relative to free CRL. It also indicated high stability under varying experimental avenues regarding temperature (ranging from 20 to $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ) and pH (ranging from 5 to 8). Shikha et al. [54] applied a rational structure-directed immobilization strategy to develop high performance and durable nanozyme bioconjugate. They fabricated aminecoated gold nanoparticles to create linkage with the carboxyl groups (aspartic and glutamic acids) of lipase enzyme from Burkholderia cepacia. Structural analysis of immobilized lipase identified the involvement of acidic amino acids at the surface of lipase. The ${\sf A u N P-N H}_{2}$ immobilized lipase has desired improved attributes, including storage and temperature stability. Lipase was immobilized onto cysteinecoated green silver nanoparticles $20\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which were prepared under alkaline conditions using green tea extract as the reducing agent. The surface of the synthesized AGNPs was activated with glutaraldehyde for the efficient immobilization of lipase. After the immobilization process, lipase bio-conjugates showed satisfactory storage stability for 2 months without a decrease in activity and retained more than $85\%$ of its original activity after five repeated runs [55]. Immobilization of lipase from a mutated strain of Fusarium incarnatum KU377454 $\mathsf{A u@A g}$ core shells nanoparticles exhibited $95\%$ of catalytic activity after storing for 15 days at $4^{\circ}\mathsf C.$ Afterward, Au@Ag conjugated enzyme was applied to degrade waste cooking oil, where it presented $85\%$ degradation of waste cooking oil than that to the free form of lipase and can be recycled in 5 consecutive cycles by retaining its activity [56].
Recently, zinc oxide nanoparticles (ZnO-NPs) turn out to be one of the most popular metal oxide nanostructures, which are widely used in catalysis, industry, cosmetics, drug delivery, and medicine due to their exceptional biocompatibility, optical, catalytic, mechanical, electrical and antimicrobial properties. ZnO-NPs are endowed with a unique capability of promoting faster electron relocation between the substrates and catalytic sites of enzymes and thus serve as a promising material in biocatalysis and enzyme engineering. Zhang et al. [57] integrated ZnO-NPs with GO to develop a novel carrier for lipase immobilization via intermolecular interactions i.e., complementary electrostatic forces or hydrogen bonding. Fig. 10 depicts the preparation of GO-coated ZnO-NPs as supporting material for the immobilization of CRL. The as-synthesized $_{\mathrm{GO/ZnO@CRL}}$ lipase system expressed outstanding performance regarding reusability and thermostability. After an incubation of $30\mathrm{min}$ at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the free form, GO-adsorbed, and $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ attached lipases dropped $64\%$ , $62\%$ , and $41\%$ of their original activities, respectively. Nevertheless, $G0/Z\mathrm{nO}@\mathrm{CRL}$ represented $63\%$ of its initial activity after heating at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ . Over $90\%$ of the activity of GO/ $Z_{\mathrm{{nO@CRL}}}$ was recorded after 14 continuous reuse cycles, whereas $Z_{\mathrm{{nO@CRL}}}$ and ${\cal G O}\ @{\cal C}\mathbb{R}\mathbb{L}$ preparation could only retain $23\%$ and $28\%$ of activity, respectively, under the same conditions.
## 6. Magnetic nanoparticles for lipase immobilization
Magnetic nanoparticles have gained increasing importance in catalysis, biological sciences, nanomedicine, and enzyme immobilization owing to their unique properties, including the larger surface area, the smaller size of the core, potent surface adsorption capability, low toxicity, better suspension properties, superparamagnetic performance, and easy separation and recycling by the use of a simple magnetic source [58]. Magnetite $\left(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}\right)$ is one of the most widely exploited magnetic support for the immobilization of lipase enzyme. However, the vulnerability of bare $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ to air oxidation and acidic settings and the inability of pure magnetite to efficiently interact with the biomolecules (enzymes or proteins) hinder their application. Thus, several surface engineering strategies have been proposed that can address the abovestated shortcomings and upgrade the enzyme-loading capacity of MNPs. Moreover, modified MNPs may facilitate site-specific covalent immobilization of enzymes, which enhances enzyme activity, stability, and reusability. Owing to convenient fabrication, non-toxicity, chemical stability, and non-carcinogenicity, many researchers have used functionalized MNPs for the immobilization of lipase for various kinds of applications (Table 5) [57,59,60].
Amino silanes such as (3-aminopropyl)triethoxysilane (APTES) are the most extensively utilized amino-functional reagents for the surface modification of MNPs. These silanes can be easily coordinated with the amino group on the surface of MNPs, creating a core-shell structure. In the presence of a bifunctional reagent such as glutaraldehyde (GA), lipases can be covalently attached to the amino group of the nanocarrier to develop a magnetic nanobiocatalyst [61]. Miao et al. [62] synthesized superparamagnetic $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ nanoparticles by the chemical coprecipitation method and surface modified with APTES. The asprepared amino-coated MNPs were employed as support material for covalent attachment of lipase enzyme in the glutaraldehyde as a cross-linker. Under optimized conditions of a catalyst dose $(20\%)$ , the methanol-oil molar ratio (6:1), and water content $(2\%)$ , the immobilized magnetic nanobiocatalyst achieved $89.4\%$ conversion of fatty acid methyl ester (FAME) at $45^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . It showed over $70\%$ of FAME conversion efficiency even after recycling for five consecutive runs. Moreover, the immobilized lipase can be easily separated by applying an external magnetic field. Lipase enzyme wad immobilized onto APTES-functionalized MNPs by environmentally friendly and biocompatible cross-linking agents 1-Ethyl-3-(Dimethylamino Propyl) Carbodiimide (EDC) and N-Hydroxysuccinimide (NHS). The resultant nanoimmobilized enzyme was used to produce biodiesel from the conversion of Aspergillus lipid. The immobilized form of lipase can be preserved for 2 months without loss of any noticeable activity and showed reusability up to 5 continuous cycles [63]. In contrast to the direct modification of pristine $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4},$ the introduction to the silicon layer could furnish a large of silanol groups, which subsequently react with organosilicon compounds more efficiently [64]. In this connection, Thangaraj et al. [65] prepared mesoporous silicon-coated $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ nanoparticles, modified with APTES or (3-mercaptopropyl) trimethoxysilane (MPTMS), and used for lipase immobilization using glutaraldehyde (Fig. 11). The efficiency of lipase immobilized on APTES-modified MNPs to convert soybean oil to biodiesel was found better than that of MPTMS modified MNPs.
Magnetic NPs produced by co-precipitation were functionalized with carboxylic acid groups using oleic acid (OA-NP), succinic anhydride (SA-NP), and citric acid (CA-NP). Lipase B from Candida Antarctica was immobilized onto these tailor-made surfaces by two different immobilization methods. These procedures entail the physical adsorption of lipase on OA-NP and carbodiimide-driven covalent attachment of lipase on SA-NP and CA-NP (Fig. 12) [66]. Generally, both the tested immobilization procedures afforded the high loading of lipase. The adsorbed CALBEDA@OA-NPs nanocatalyst showed the maximum activity, while the covalently bound CALBEDA@SA-NPs and CALBEDA@CA-NPs were found to be thermostable. They also revealed the excellent enantioselectivity in the catalytic kinetic resolution reaction [66].
In biopolymers, polydopamine and chitosan have found wide utilization for the modification of magnetic nanomaterials. Zhang group coated a mesoporous polydopamine layer on the surface of the hollow MNPs by adopting a fast interface-directed co-assembly method. In comparison to bare MNPs, the as-prepared hollow MNPs showed low density, high magnetization, rigid pore structures, adjustable pore sizes, substantial specific surface area, and decent monodispersity.
Based on their unique properties, lipase was effectively conjugated on the walls of polydopamine-coated hollow MNPs and used to synthesize biodiesel. Using the optimized esterification conditions, the immobilized enzyme exhibited a conversion of $87.9\%$ and retained over $71.3\%$ of its activity after six repeated cycles [67]. Chitosan, poly [b-(1-4)-linked-2-amino-2-deoxy-D-glucose], displays desirable properties of good biocompatibility, biodegradability, non-toxicity, mucus adhesion, and mild preparation conditions [68]. It can be employed as a choice biomaterial for magnetic carriers due to the presence of a large number of functional moieties that can be tunable for targetspecific applications [69]. Two types of strategies for magnetic chitosan synthesis include one-step and two-steps synthesis methods. One-step synthesis strategy entails the in-situ preparation of MNPs in chitosan solution, whereas, in a two-step synthesis approach, first MNPs are separately prepared and then treated with chitosan for modification [70,71]. Enzymes are usually immobilized on magnetic nanochitosan by covalent binding. Xie and Wang [72] reported the immobilization of Candida rugosa lipase onto high-quality magnetic chitosan beads, which were developed by the chemical co-precipitation method. The resulted immobilized enzyme showed an $87\%$ transformation of soybean oil into FAME and could be reusable for four repeated times without loss of any significant activity.
Graphene oxide (GO) bears a vast number of functional moieties on its surface, such as epoxides, carboxyl, hydroxyl groups, which render it useful for MNPs modification. Therefore, GO-modified MNPs have been exploited as support carriers for the immobilization of lipases. Xie and Huang [26] carried out the encapsulation of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ in GO to fabricate a GO-based magnetic nanocomposite. The amino group of lipases was covalently attached to the carboxyl group present on GO using NHS and EDC as cross-linkers. Immobilized magnetic lipase achieved $92.8\%$ of biodiesel yield from soybean oil and could be recovered and recycled 5 times without dropping a substantial activity.
Cross-linking enzyme aggregates (CLEAs) is an exquisitely simple, inexpensive, and broadly applicable support-free immobilization technique that has numerous benefits in the perspective of biocatalysis. CLEAs preparation encompasses the precipitation or aggregation of enzymes from aqueous buffer followed by a bifunctional reagent-assisted cross-linking of the resultant aggregate enzyme [73]. Nevertheless, an increase in the size of the CLEAs cluster by separation from the reaction system following filtration or centrifugation results in high mass transfer resistances. Additionally, the marginal mechanical strength of CLEAs may hinder their broader industrial applicability. The development of magnetic CLEAs by the immobilization of CLEAs onto MNPs addresses these problems (Fig. 13) [74]. Easy separation and recovery of magnetic CLEAs (mCLEAs) with magnets circumvents irretrievable aggregation of CLEAs induced by centrifugation or filtration. In addition, mCLEAs exhibit considerably improved shelf life and working stability and are stable towards leaching in the aqueous milieu [75]. Badoei-dalfard et al. [76] synthesized Bacillus licheniformis $\mathrm{Km}12$ Lipase $\mathrm{Km}12$ lipase magnetic CLEAs by covalent coupling of lipase on amino-coated MNPs (Fig. 14). The as-prepared mCLEAs-lipase nanoconstructs preserved its complete activity up to six continuous reuse cycles and retained over $60\%$ of its original activity after incubating at $4~^{\circ}C$ for 24 days. The immobilized enzyme showed $20\%$ increased biodiesel synthesis from waste cooking oils compared with the free form of the enzyme. The same authors immobilized CLEAs-lipase onto magnetic amino-coated GO nanocomposites [77], which presented a wide working range of temperature than that to the free form of the enzyme. The engineered maGO-CLEAs-lip nanocues showed the highest enzyme activity up to five successive reuse runs and reserved over $70\%$ of its primary activity after incubating for one month. In contrast to the free enzyme, the biodiesel yield of maGO-CLEAs-lipase was 3-folds greater that makes it a versatile nanobiocatalyst for industrial applications [77]. $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ MNPs produced by the precipitation of ferric ion $(\mathsf{F e}^{3+})$ and ferrous ion $(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+})$ in alkaline solution were treated with tannic acid and used for the immobilization of lipase by two methods. In the first technique, the novel type of tannic acid-modified lipase m-CLEAs (TA-MNPsCLEAs) was developed by cross-linking of lipase aggregates and starch (as co-feeder) with TA-improved MNPs (TA-MNPs). The lipase was attached in the second phase to the surface of TA-MNPs. Both the newly developed bioconjugates TA-MNPs-lipase and TA-MNPs-CLEAs-starch lipase displayed high stability and catalytic performance to produce biodiesel. The immobilized magnetic nanobiocatalyst could be efficiently recovered from the solution and recycles in multiple cycles [78].
## 7. Metal-organic frameworks for lipase immobilization
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a popular class of porous materials that have become a rising star in the past two decades. MOFs have gained application in an array of various research fields thanks to their exceptional set of features, including huge surface area, versatility, crystallinity, and structural diversity and designability, etc. [79–81]. Moreover, MOFs have also drawn considerable research interest as a novel platform for immobilizing various enzymes. It has been revealed that many MOFenzyme bioconjugates displayed a much-improved catalytic performance and manipulative stability and recycling efficiency [82,83]. Typically, three strategies, namely surface immobilization, enzyme encapsulation within MOFs, and pore adsorption (diffusion of enzyme molecules into the pores of MOFs) are used to constitute enzyme-MOF biocomposites [84]. Surface immobilization is among the most widely applied method for the immobilization of lipase onto MOFs because of the challenging lipases accessibility to micropores of the majority of the MOFs. However, this approach usually does not impart satisfactory stability improvements to the immobilized biocatalysts [85].
A wide range of MOFs has been designed for the encapsulation of lipase enzymes (Table 6). Surfactants-activated lipase from Aspergillus niger was encapsulated/entrapped within ZIF-8 via a facile one-pot self-assembly technique. The developed SDS-coated ZIF-8- lipase displayed $250\%$ augmented activity relative to the free counterpart. Similarly, the thermal stability profile revealed higher than 2-folds improved stability with respect to half-life for prepared SDS-modified ZIF-8-lipase biocomposite was achieved in a temperature range of $50{-}70^{\circ}\mathsf C.$ It also reserved over $75\%$ and $91\%$ of remaining activity after catalyzing six repeated catalytic and storage for 20 days. In contrast to free lipase, immobilized biocatalyst showed excellent chemical stability in the denaturation of polar solvents [86]. Cai et al. [87] developed a novel MOF encapsulation and macroporous resin adsorption method for CALB immobilization. Lipase enzyme was first encapsulated in ZIF8, followed by binding to macroporous resin via physical adsorption by the addition of polyethyleneimine (Fig. 15) [87]. Compared to the free form of the enzyme, the ZIF-8-immobilized lipase demonstrated increased operational and thermal stability, as well as resistance to organic solvents. In another study, hierarchical ordered ZIF-8 structures with high surface area, good crystallinity, and thermostability were used for encapsulating Burkholderia cepacia lipase. The resultant $\mathtt{B C L@}$ MAC-ZIF-8 manifested raised enzyme activity and recoverable for multiple consecutive hydrolytic runs [88].
Though several enzymes have been robustly attached to the MPFs surface by physical adsorption, adsorbed biocatalysts associated via only weak interactions between the enzyme molecules and MOFs often display low stability [89]. These weak interactions could be either intermolecular or intramolecular that play a central role in the construction of nanostructured materials, such as the supramolecular architecture of MOFs, as well as in enzyme engineering for the improvement of the catalytic efficiency [90,91]. Covalent attachment of lipase onto MOFs is an efficient approach to augment stability attributes. It is eloquent to illustrate that the formation of various covalent linkages between the carrier support and enzyme reduces conformational flexibility and firmly held the enzyme preventing protein unfolding or denaturation [92]. A vast number of functional moieties (e.g., carboxyl, amino, hydroxyl groups) on the surface of MOFs can be functionalized to facilitate their connection with reactive groups of the enzyme. Zou et al. [93] used the monomer of the ZIF-8 (2-methyl imidazole-4-carboxylic acid) as a chemical modification agent for CRL, which exhibited 1.3-times higher catalytic activity compared to the free state of the enzyme. The modified CRL was then immobilized in the ZIF-8 skeleton via the innovative monomer to develop immobilized CRL composite. Immobilization secures the active center of the enzyme from destruction, and immobilized lipase retains the same catalytic activity as CRL immobilized through physical embedding. All the desired attributes ascertained, including storage stability, reusability, and resistance to organic solvents, were significantly augmented. Modified immobilized CRL presented a substantial enhancement in tolerance to isopropanol, retaining over $148\%$ of its actual activity. It also preserved $52.3\%$ of activity after seven repeated uses, while the remaining activity of the nonmodified form of CRL was only about $40\%$ . A series of MOFs-lipase conjugates were obtained by successful immobilization of Pseudomonas fluorescens lipase (PFL) onto MOFs by the covalent cross-linking method and applied to transesterification of 4-methoxymandelic acid enantiomers and enantioselective hydrolysis of 2-(4-hydroxyphenyl) propionic acid ethyl ester enantiomers in the organic and aqueous medium, respectively. According to the experiments, PFL@Uio-66(Zr) achieved excellent enantioselectivities and high catalytic performances. To further enhance biocatalytic activity and recycling efficiency, PFL was treated with PEG and subsequently immobilized onto Uio-66(Zr), yielding PFL-PEG@Uio-66(Zr) preparation that presented superior catalytic activity and enhanced reusability than that to PFL@Uio-66(Zr) [94].
## 8. Hybrid nanoflowers lipase immobilization
Protein conjugated inorganic hybrid nanoflowers were first discovered by Ge et al. [95] by a rapid and facile synthesis method. In this hybrid nanoflowers conjugate, the combination of proteins with metal ions constitutes flower-shaped nanostructures in the presence of $\mathsf{C u P O}_{4}$ as an inorganic component under favorable environments. Due to the provision of enhanced stability and activity to the catalyst, this fascinating method has attracted accelerating attention and widely adopted to immobilize a large number of industrially relevant enzyme, including $\upalpha$ -chymotrypsin [96], lipase (Table 7) [97], $\upalpha$ -amylase [98], horseradish peroxidase [99], catalase [100], papain [101], and trypsin [102] using various metals as the inorganic constituent. The majority of as-synthesized inorganic hybrid nanoflowers immobilized enzyme presented improved biocatalytic activity and thermal stability characteristics. Zhang et al. [104] created a novel lipase conjugated $\mathsf{Z C}12/\mathsf{C a}_{3}$ $(\mathsf{P O}_{4})_{2}$ hybrid flowers-like nanoflowers that expressed aggrandized enzyme activity $(206\%)$ and 2.31-fold in catalytic efficiency at low temperature than that to free enzyme. It demonstrating superior reusability retaining its initial activity after seven consecutive reaction batches. The stability profile revealed no change in activity over a broad range of pH and temperature. Finally, it showed double fructose laurate conversion activity $(57.39\%)$ compared to the free form of the enzyme. Lee et al. [105] proposed a facile and highly effective strategy for the synthesis of hybrid nanoflowers, which were treated with glutaraldehyde to crosslink enzyme molecules trapped within the hybrid nanoflowers. For this, the reaction of CRL with $\mathsf{C u P O}_{4}$ for 3 days and then treatment with glutaraldehyde for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ successfully synthesized GA-functionalized lipase nanoflowers, which have flower-shaped morphologies. Such GA-modified lipase nanoflowers maintained over $70\%$ of their initial activities after four repeated cycles, together with the preservation of flower-like architecture. On the other hand, the conventional lipase-immobilized nanoflowers were impractical for reusability and dropped most of their initial activities (about $90\%$ ) after four consecutive runs. Aydemir et al. [106] introduced a triple enzyme-based inorganic hybrid flower-like nanostructure (TrpE@ihNFs) by amalgamating three different commonly used industrial enzymes, including lipase, protease, and a-amylase with ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ ions as the inorganic constituent. Comparative evaluation of the enzyme stability and activity of this hybrid nanoconjugate revealed significantly higher enzyme activities and stabilities of the immobilized enzyme at different pH and temperatures than that to corresponding free form of enzymes. Despite the increased surface area, magnified activity, and stability, difficulties in the separation of lipase-immobilized inorganic hybrid nanoflowers hamper their applications. In this connection, Ren et al. [107] synthesized magnetic lipase- $\left.\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{P O}_{4})_{2}\right.$ hybrid nanoflowers by adopting the co-precipitation. Using a magnetic field, the resultant magnetic nanoflowers were quickly recovered from the reaction combination. In contrast to the free enzyme, the MNFs $@$ lipase conjugates displayed better enzymatic activity and augmented $\mathsf{p H}(\mathsf{p H}10.0)$ and thermal stability $(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ . Furthermore, the magnetic nanobiocatalyst was efficiently applied to the solvent-free production of benzyl acetate through vinyl acetate and benzyl alcohol.
## 9. Conclusions and prospects
Enzyme immobilization has acquired the prime position as an engineering approach with a huge interest in academia, the scientific community, and industrial sectors. Thanks to the technological advancements, an array of different nanosize materials and combinations of these materials has arisen as appealing choices for lipases immobilization. Unique attributes, including tiny particle size, large accessible surface area, and high enzyme loading capability turn out nanoscale materials as an emerging type of candidate to develop immobilized lipase-based biocatalytic systems. A wealth of new nanoscale support materials such as graphene/graphene oxide, carbon nanotubes, nano-silica, metal nanoparticles, electrospun nanofibers, magnetic nanostructures, metal-organic frameworks, and hybrid nanoflowers are being studied as a host matrix to immobilize lipase enzymes. Notably, immobilized lipases onto nanomaterials usually demonstrate improved catalytic properties, enhanced productivity, and long-term storage stability and reusability. Though the scale-up production and applicability of immobilized biocatalysts is a big challenge, immobilization is an enabling tool that might constitute enzyme commercially available for practical applications. Fabrication of unique porous nanomaterials that can incorporate enzyme molecules in the pores might be a noteworthy approach. Furthermore, magnetic nanostructures can be considered as more promising carrier supports for lipases immobilization owing to their easy separation, recovery, and reusability properties. This article focusses on the utilization of nanomaterials for immobilizing lipases to improve their catalytic properties, productivity, and reducing processing cost. In the future, it would be meaningful to carry out the overall cost evaluation of nanobiocatalysts. Though the nano-biocatalysts trim down the enzyme cost due to high catalytic performance and reusability, it raises the cost of downstream processing/separation and nanomaterials used. These are the significant disadvantages that may hinder the large-scale applicability of nano-biocatalysts. In this connection, future research efforts should be devoted to fabricating easily recyclable and low-cost nanosupports. The design and development of immobilized enzymebased bioreactor, such as packed bed bioreactor, microchannel reactor, magnetically stabilized fluidized bed bioreactor, etc., offers a possible way to reduce costs. Still, most of the nanobiocatalyst research has so far endeavored to small reaction scale. In consideration of the abovediscussed points, the requirements that allow applying nanosize materials technology on a larger scale is still in its preliminary stage, and further in-depth studies are needed, especially using continuous reactors. In this way, enzyme-based nanobiocatalyst can be extended to larger industrial sectors as an environmentally friendly tool.
